# Hunting the slide: the session bench

Building a bench that behaves like a rumble client

Unit 3, talk 3

---

## What no bench had ever done

Every bench pass, including BENCH-4, forked a **fresh JVM for each battle** and met at most **60** opponents.

A rumble client does the opposite:

- every battle in **one** `RobocodeEngine` process
- a heap capped at `-Xmx512M`
- **hundreds** of distinct opponents in a row

That difference had never been benched (L-24).

---

## The session bench (BENCH-6, BENCH-7)

- 300 battles, one engine process, 512 MB heap, data directory kept
- 35 rounds a battle, reported in blocks of 25
- A **control robot** (`sample.Tracker`) fights the same opponents in the same process, so anything wrong with the host shows on both

---

## The result

| Battles | Weak survival | Skipped turns / battle | Heap after GC |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1-25 | 100.0% | 10.0 | 7 MB |
| 101-125 | 99.7% | 39.5 | 6 MB |
| 201-225 | 98.7% | 67.3 | 6 MB |
| 276-300 | 99.5% | **89.6** | 6 MB |

The control skipped 0.0 to 0.1 turns a battle in every block. No slide in survival or score. A steady climb in skipped turns, Hadur only.

---

## Where the skips were

- Every added skip sat **one tick after a round ended**
- Battle 299: 164 skipped turns, **160** of them at round end + 1
- Heap, live classes and turn p95 (about 1 ms) were flat
- So nothing per tick grew. Only the one stall per round did.

---

## The bisect

Same session, but the data directory **wiped before every battle** (150 battles):

- Skipped turns per battle stayed flat, 7.1 in the first block and 8.2 in the last
- So the growth needs the accumulating data directory

A prefilled directory reproduced it, on both the 3.2 and 3.3 jars.

---

## The cost, by size of directory

| Profiles on disk | Skipped turns (3.3) | Save |
|---|---|---|
| 0 | 4 | written |
| 300 | 10 | written without seeds |
| 1000 | 57 | skipped |
| 2000 | 96 | skipped |

Past about 700 stats-only profiles (275 bytes each, 200 KB quota), **every save is skipped** and Hadur stops learning (L-27).

---

## The cause

`ProfileLibrary.save` did work proportional to the number of files on disk:

1. walk the directory to add up the bytes used
2. above 90% of quota, read and decode **every** profile to find the ones holding seeds
3. then skip the write anyway

Each file goes through the security manager, so 1,000 to 2,000 files meant a stall of 100 to 200 ms.

---

## A planner checks an implementer

The skipped-turns plan re-checked the implementer's diagnosis against the raw session logs and the Robocode engine source. It confirmed the cause and corrected four points before any code was written.

One correction: three engine disables in the session were Hadur's, mid-round, and **unexplained** (L-28).

---

## What it did not explain

- The stalls happen after the round is decided and cost **no rounds** on the bench
- So on the bench this was a real fault, but the plan did not call it the live slide: "not the whole of the live gap"
- 3.4's live pass later showed no slide (next talk)
- 3.3 added duress mode (RES-9): after three skipped turns in a round, stop learning and play a cheap orbit

---

## What it taught us

- If the bench cannot reproduce a problem, copy the failing environment more closely
- A control robot turns "something is slow" into "it is us"
- A bisect (change one thing, keep the rest) pointed at the data directory
- A fix can be right and still not be the fix you wanted. Write down which one it is.

---

